  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH SUNDAY...After some morning stratus, skies have \nstarted to clear out so have a mix of sun and clouds across the \nregion. Where skies were clear, temperatures were in the mid to \nupper 80s, but where the clouds lingered, temperatures were in \nthe upper 70s to lower 80s. For the rest of the day, expect dry\nconditions with light winds. \n\nLater tonight, expect scattered thunderstorms to develop across\nthe eastern Dakotas into western MN on the leading edge of some\nH850 theta_e advection. These should weaken as they approach the\nforecast area, but additional development is expected in the\nafternoon and evening along the axis of instability across\nwestern MN. These storms could produce gusty winds and hail, and\nthe Marginal Risk (1 out of 5) remains across western Minnesota.\nThese storms should weaken as they move east, so not\nanticipating them to cross the I-35 corridor.\n\nMONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY...On Monday, a potent upper level wave\nwill kick across the High Plains, and bring widespread\nprecipitation across international border closer to the surface\nlow. A trailing cold front south of this low will pass through\nthe region, and this should bring precipitation to most\nlocations from Monday into Tuesday morning. But given the\ntiming, the storms will probably be at their weakest and moving\nfairly quick, so not anticipating soaking rains. A few of the\nstrongest storms could produce damaging winds or large hail, and\nSPC maintains the marginal risk.\n\nRain will exit the region Tuesday morning, and any redevelopment\nlater in the day should be to the east across Wisconsin. On\nWednesday, breezy west winds of 25 to 30 mph will develop in the\nwake of the cold front. Highs will only be in the 70s. Looking\nahead, there are small chances for storms later in the week and\nnext weekend.",
